+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,268 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `csv` / `csv-core`
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest`
+0.28.2) to the existing unit-test modules of both workspace members. There
+was no pre-existing proptest/quickcheck/fuzz setup to port; the closest
+existing thing is csv-core's `parses_to!` macro, which compares the DFA and
+NFA parsers on canned inputs — property 2 below generalizes it.
+
+`cargo add --dev hegeltest` required `--ignore-rust-version` in the root
+package (csv pins `rust-version = "1.73"`, hegeltest needs 1.86). If these
+tests are upstreamed, that dev-dependency MSRV is a maintainer decision.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Ten distinct properties (plus six deterministic "known failure" tests that
+pin real bugs, listed in the next section):
+
+1. **Write→read round-trip with generated configurations**
+   (`src/writer.rs`, `hegel_write_read_roundtrip_with_generated_config`).
+   Generates a *valid* configuration (delimiter, quote, CRLF/Any
+   terminator, double-quote vs escape mode, comment char, quote style
+   Necessary/Always/NonNumeric) with the validity rules — pairwise-distinct
+   special bytes, NonNumeric only when no special byte can occur inside a
+   numeric field — encoded as construction logic, plus records of arbitrary
+   bytes (including delimiters, quotes, newlines, invalid UTF-8), writes
+   them and reads them back with the matching reader configuration.
+   Evidence: `Writer` docs ("Quote fields when necessary"), `QuoteStyle`
+   docs, the `comment` builder docs. *Found bug 1.*
+
+2. **DFA/NFA parser equivalence** (`src/reader.rs`,
+   `hegel_dfa_nfa_equivalence`). For arbitrary (including degenerate)
+   reader configurations and CSV-ish byte inputs, the default DFA parser
+   and the debug NFA parser (`ReaderBuilder::nfa(true)`) must produce
+   identical records, positions and errors. Evidence: csv-core's own
+   `parses_to!` unit tests assert exactly this on canned inputs. *Found
+   bugs 2, 3, 4, 5.*
+
+3. **Position/seek round-trip** (`src/reader.rs`,
+   `hegel_position_seek_roundtrip`). `position()` captured before each
+   record can be `seek`ed to and yields the same record and all subsequent
+   records, including their positions. Evidence: `Reader::position` doc
+   ("seeking to a position returned here on the same data will result in
+   parsing the same subsequent record").
+
+4. **Flexible vs strict record lengths** (`src/reader.rs`,
+   `hegel_flexible_vs_strict_record_lengths`). With `flexible(true)`
+   variable-width records round-trip unchanged; with the strict default,
+   exactly the records whose width differs from the first record's width
+   yield `UnequalLengths` with the documented `expected_len`/`len` values.
+   Evidence: `ReaderBuilder::flexible` docs, `ReaderState::add_record`.
+
+5. **Reader robustness on arbitrary input** (`src/reader.rs`,
+   `hegel_reader_never_panics_on_arbitrary_input`). Any configuration
+   (including `trim`, header handling and the UTF-8-validating
+   `StringRecord` path) on arbitrary bytes: no panics, byte positions never
+   decrease and never exceed input length, `is_done()` after exhaustion.
+   Evidence: parse-robustness pattern; `is_done` doc example.
+
+6. **ByteRecord model test** (`src/byte_record.rs`,
+   `hegel_byte_record_model`, `#[hegel::state_machine]`). `ByteRecord`
+   against a `Vec<Vec<u8>>` model under `push_field`/`truncate`/`clear`/
+   `trim`, with an invariant checking `len`, `is_empty`, `get`, `iter`,
+   `range` and `as_slice` agree with the model after every rule. Evidence:
+   method docs (e.g. `truncate` beyond length "has no effect", `range` and
+   `as_slice` consistency).
+
+7. **ByteRecord::trim matches its documented whitespace class**
+   (`src/byte_record.rs`, `hegel_trim_matches_documented_class`), with
+   whitespace-biased field content. Evidence: `trim` doc: "only bytes in
+   the class `[\t\n\v\f\r ]` are trimmed". *Found bug 6.*
+
+8. **StringRecord::trim rebuild oracle + position + idempotence**
+   (`src/string_record.rs`,
+   `hegel_trim_rebuilds_fields_and_preserves_position`). Trimming a record
+   of arbitrary Unicode fields equals a record rebuilt from per-field
+   `str::trim` results (exercises the internal bounds rebuild), preserves
+   the record position, and is idempotent.
+
+9. **csv-core streaming writer ≡ one-shot writer**
+   (`csv-core/src/writer.rs`, `hegel_streaming_writes_equal_oneshot`).
+   Writing the same records through 2–5-byte output buffers, resuming on
+   `OutputFull`, produces byte-identical output to one big buffer, for
+   arbitrary configurations (all four quote styles, arbitrary and even
+   colliding special bytes). Evidence: resumability contract in the
+   `field`/`delimiter`/`terminator`/`finish` docs (chunk ≥ 2 because of the
+   documented two-byte atomic writes).
+
+10. **csv-core reader is chunking-independent** (`csv-core/src/reader.rs`,
+    `hegel_chunked_input_equals_whole_input`). `read_record` fed the whole
+    input vs the same bytes split at arbitrary boundaries (with small
+    output/ends buffers, exercising `OutputFull`/`OutputEndsFull` resumes)
+    yields the same records. Evidence: the streaming contract in the
+    `read_record` docs. The documented BOM-buffering limitation
+    (`strip_utf8_bom`: "will fail to strip off the BOM if only part of the
+    BOM is buffered") is respected by keeping a leading BOM inside the
+    first chunk.
+
+Liveness was verified by temporarily inverting/corrupting an assertion in
+every property that had not already failed for real (all reported shrunk
+counterexamples) and reverting. An exploratory run at `test_cases = 1000`
+(10x) was done for both packages — it found one additional trigger of bug 3
+(the `OutputEndsFull` route) that the default count missed — and the counts
+were then reverted to the default.
+
+## Test results
+
+Final state (`cargo test` in root, `cargo test -p csv-core`):
+
+- csv lib tests: **149 passed, 6 failed** — the 6 failures are exactly the
+  deterministic KNOWN FAILURE tests pinning real bugs (below).
+- csv integration tests (42) and doc tests (138): all pass.
+- csv-core: **102 passed, 0 failed** (includes the 2 hegel properties).
+
+All ten properties above are green (also at 10x case count); every pinned
+bug test fails on every run with a fixed, minimal-shape input.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+Six pinned failing tests document five distinct bugs (bug 6 is a
+doc/implementation contradiction):
+
+1. **Escape byte in field data does not round-trip with
+   `double_quote(false)`**
+   (`writer::tests::hegel_roundtrip_escape_byte_in_field_known_bug`).
+   Minimal counterexample (hegel-shrunk): delimiter=0, quote=1,
+   escape=Some(29), field `[29]` → encoded `[1, 29, 1, 13, 10]`
+   (`"<esc>"`) → read back as `[[1, 13, 10]]`. The writer escapes *quotes*
+   with the escape byte but never escapes the escape byte itself
+   (`csv_core::writer::quote` only searches for the quote byte);
+   `WriterBuilder::build` even sets `requires_quotes[escape]`, but quoting
+   cannot help because the reader interprets the escape byte inside quoted
+   fields and takes the next byte literally. Any field containing the
+   escape byte is corrupted on read-back. Code path:
+   `csv-core/src/writer.rs` `quote()` + `csv-core/src/reader.rs`
+   `InQuotedField`/`InEscapedQuote`.
+
+2. **`nfa(true)` corrupts records whose output spans multiple `read_record`
+   calls**
+   (`reader::tests::hegel_nfa_corrupts_records_spanning_output_buffers_known_bug`).
+   Reading `aaaaa` (no trailing newline) with
+   `ReaderBuilder::new().nfa(true)` yields `aaaa\0`. `read_record_nfa`
+   starts writing at `output[self.output_pos]` and returns a cumulative
+   `nout`, while `read_record_dfa` writes from offset 0 of the caller's
+   buffer and returns per-call counts (using `output_pos` only for `ends`
+   values). The `csv` crate's `read_byte_record_impl` advances the output
+   window between calls (the DFA convention), so on the NFA path bytes land
+   past their correct offsets whenever `output_pos > 0` at call entry.
+   Code path: `csv-core/src/reader.rs` `read_record_nfa` vs
+   `read_record_dfa`; `src/reader.rs` `read_byte_record_impl`.
+
+3. **Same root cause, `OutputEndsFull` route** — found only by the 10x
+   exploratory run: delimiter-heavy records overflow the `ends` buffer
+   mid-record, leaving `output_pos > 0`, and the next NFA call again writes
+   past the window (shrunk counterexample: delimiter=terminator=0, input
+   `[1,0,0,0,0,13]` → DFA `[[1],[],[],[],[13]]`, NFA `[[1],[],[],[],[0]]`).
+   Documented in the same pinned test's comment; the equivalence property
+   avoids it by pre-sizing both record buffers.
+
+4. **`nfa(true)` never updates line numbers**
+   (`reader::tests::hegel_nfa_does_not_track_line_numbers_known_bug`).
+   Only the DFA paths increment `self.line`; `read_record_nfa`/
+   `read_field_nfa` never do, so every position (including positions inside
+   `UnequalLengths` errors) reports line 1. Code path:
+   `csv-core/src/reader.rs` (grep `self.line +=` — DFA only).
+
+5. **Trailing comment line yields a phantom empty record on the default
+   (DFA) path**
+   (`reader::tests::hegel_trailing_comment_line_yields_phantom_record_known_bug`).
+   With a completely ordinary config, reading `"a,b\n# trailing comment"`
+   with `comment(Some(b'#'))` yields `[["a", "b"], [""]]` — and with the
+   strict default the phantom record even surfaces as a spurious
+   `UnequalLengths` error. Root cause: `transition_final_dfa` emits a
+   record for *any* non-start, non-final state at EOF, including
+   `InComment`; `transition_final_nfa` correctly maps `InComment` to `End`.
+   Code path: `csv-core/src/reader.rs` `transition_final_dfa`.
+
+   Related, found while shrinking: with `Terminator::Any(_)` the DFA and
+   NFA also disagree on where a comment *ends*
+   (`reader::tests::hegel_dfa_nfa_disagree_on_comments_with_any_terminator_known_bug`):
+   `transition_nfa` hardcodes `b'\n'` as the comment exit, but `build_dfa`
+   only adds `\r`/`\n` to the byte equivalence classes for CRLF
+   terminators, so on the DFA path a comment never ends at `\n` and
+   swallows the rest of the input. (That comments ignore an `Any(t)`
+   terminator entirely and only end at `\n` is itself surprising behavior,
+   but DFA-vs-NFA disagreement is the clear-cut contract violation pinned
+   here.)
+
+6. **`ByteRecord::trim` documentation contradicts the implementation on
+   `\v`** (`byte_record::tests::hegel_trim_documented_class_known_bug`).
+   The doc says "only bytes in the class `[\t\n\v\f\r ]` are trimmed", but
+   the implementation uses `u8::is_ascii_whitespace`, which excludes `\v`
+   (0x0B): a field `[0x0B]` is not trimmed. Notably the existing unit test
+   `trim_whitespace_only` exercises `" \t\n\r\x0c"` — every documented byte
+   except `\v`. Fix is a one-word doc change or a one-byte impl change.
+   Code path: `src/byte_record.rs` `trim`/`trim_ascii` + doc comment.
+
+Per instructions, no bugs were fixed; each is pinned by a deterministic
+failing test, and sibling properties exclude only the pinned inputs (each
+exclusion carries a comment naming the canonical test).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+No property was abandoned outright, but two were narrowed after
+investigation, and the narrowing is part of the generators by design:
+
+- **Round-trip with `QuoteStyle::NonNumeric` and "numeric" special bytes**:
+  if the delimiter (or quote/terminator/comment) is a byte that can occur
+  inside a field that parses as `f64`/`i128` (digits, `+-.`, `e`,
+  `inf`/`infinity`/`nan` letters), NonNumeric leaves such fields unquoted
+  and the output is ambiguous by design. The config generator falls back to
+  `Necessary` in that case rather than asserting an impossible round-trip.
+- **Round-trip with pairwise-colliding special bytes** (e.g. delimiter ==
+  terminator): unparseable by construction, so the config generator draws
+  distinct special bytes. The csv-core equivalence/streaming properties
+  deliberately do *not* apply these constraints (any config must still be
+  chunking-independent and DFA≡NFA), which is exactly how bugs 2–5 were
+  found.
+- The **chunked-reader property** carves out the *documented* BOM-buffering
+  limitation rather than treating it as a bug (`strip_utf8_bom` explicitly
+  says partial-BOM buffering isn't handled).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked very well — the "generate configurations, not just
+payloads" section and the DFA/NFA-style equivalence pattern directly found
+five real bugs in a 10-year-old, heavily-used crate. Specific notes:
+
+1. **`no_std` guidance in the Rust reference is incomplete.** The reference
+   says to add `use std::{format, string::ToString};` to test modules in
+   `no_std` crates. In csv-core this fails with E0433 "unresolved module or
+   unlinked crate `std`" — `std` is not in the extern prelude at all. The
+   working recipe is `extern crate std;` *inside the test module*, then the
+   `use std::...` imports (also needed: `std::vec` for the `vec!` macro and
+   `std::vec::Vec`). Suggest updating the reference's no_std gotcha.
+
+2. **The 10x exploratory run earned its keep** and should stay a mandatory
+   step: the `OutputEndsFull` corruption route (bug 3) appeared only at
+   1000 cases, after the default-count suite had gone green.
+
+3. **Repeated randomized runs also earned their keep**: during the
+   liveness-check run (fresh random seeds), the un-mutated equivalence
+   property failed again because the NFA line-tracking bug leaks through a
+   second observation channel (positions embedded in `UnequalLengths`
+   errors), which the property then had to scrub explicitly. Non-CI runs
+   being freshly seeded each time is effectively free extra exploration.
+
+4. **"Exclude known-buggy input from sibling properties" scaled well** but
+   was the most delicate part of the work: one bug (NFA `output_pos`) has
+   three distinct trigger routes (`OutputFull`, `OutputEndsFull`, chunk
+   boundaries), and each needed its own accommodation in the sibling
+   property (pre-sizing both `ByteRecord` buffers). A sentence in SKILL.md
+   noting that one bug may need multiple, mechanically-different exclusions
+   (and that each exclusion should be re-validated by the 10x run) would
+   have saved an iteration.
+
+5. **Hegel's shrinking was excellent** — every real failure shrank to a
+   minimal, human-readable counterexample on the first run (e.g.
+   `escape=Some(29), field=[29]`; `input=[1,1,1,1,1]`), and the imperative
+   draw style made the "draw a byte avoiding a forbidden set" constructive
+   generator trivial to write.
+
+6. Minor: `#[hegel::composite]` requiring plain-value returns (draw inside,
+   don't return generators) and the single-use nature of generators matched
+   the reference exactly; no API surprises. The failure database replaying
+   old counterexamples across generator changes was briefly confusing
+   during iteration (a stale entry re-fails a test whose generator no
+   longer produces that input); `rm -rf .hegel` between structural edits
+   resolved it — worth a sentence in the reference's gotchas.
